Round 64 is complete and deployed. The queue check came first: August's second list has been worked in full since round 57, so the standing survey applied, and SHIPWRIGHT-QUEUE item 9 — mast bands, wooldings and the cheeks at the hounds — was the round's work.

**What shipped.** The rule is structural, from the record's own fields: a wooden square-rig lower mast drawn past 0.55 m through is past what one tree gives, so it's a made mast and must be bound. The binding is dated technology — rope wooldings pinched between wooden hoops before 1800, shrunk iron hoops after — chosen by `S.year`, the depicted year, now set for the four ships in the class (carrack 1500, East Indiaman 1620, the 74 at 1780, clipper 1869; all below the 1890 antifouling switch, so nothing else moved). Rings merge into one mesh per mast. At the masthead, `buildTop`'s old frame was one trestletree and one crosstree passing *through* the mast — a frame that could not have been assembled; it's now the real pairs, with two tapered cheek knees under every doubled masthead. Single-tier masts (cog, trireme, corbita) stay bare-headed by rule.

