The live stamp is 1786534808 — matches the final build, deploy verified. Notably, the stamp before the deploy was 1786526986, round 89's: proof that round 90 never actually deployed despite its handoff claim.

## Round 91 summary

**What this round found:** Round 90 (the sekibune, the 33rd hull) had completed all its work and written its handoff — including "post-accept run clean" and "live stamp verified" — but its process died before committing. HEAD was still at round 89, the whole tree was stranded, and the ratchet log the handoff cited was a zero-byte file.

**Landing the stranded round:** I re-ran every gate before repeating any claim — audit 33 hulls / 0 problems, site rebuilt, the full post-accept ratchet run into the same log it cited (55/55 frames green), and the sekibune's own frame read: one sail on one mast, the yakata standing over the wall, oar blades resting awash — all three of round 90's fixes visible on screen. It landed as its own commit (7c4b196), with the true story in the message.

**This round's own work — the r86 carried item, closed:** August's second list has been worked in full since round 57, so the round fell through to the carried queue. The raycast ring survey finally ran against all four oared hulls (galley, galleass, panokseon, sekibune): 72 bearings × 3 heights on two rings each — the topside band above the waterline and the gun-deck wall's own band. Result: 216/216 strikes everywhere, zero see-through bearings; every wall band sits exactly where its record derives it.

**The audit keeps the ring** as a standing rule pair — 'you can see through the gun-deck wall' and 'gun deck without a wall' — because the atakebune is already anticipated and the next gunDeck hull should be born checked. Both proven by injection: opening the panokseon's end panels convicts on 10 of 216 bearings (the corridor round 89 found by eye, now machine-visible); stripping the sekibune's wall convicts on the companion rule, with the floating-part rule joining in coherently. Clean run after: 33/0. Committed as 14fab2d.

**Flagged for August in the handoff:** round 90 is the third round killed mid-verification in two days. A full ratchet run costs ~30 of the 80 budgeted minutes, so a round that starts its verification late has no margin — worth a look alongside the pending two-writer lock decision. **Next round:** the Myeongnyang campaign in the Action — both its hulls now exist and are live.
